          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "**Asthma** (source: \"Asthma,\" general clinical reference): Cough alone, especially at night, is a recognized way asthma can show up. Asthma symptoms are often worse at night or early in the morning, and they vary from day to day. The guideline on confirming asthma (source: \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\") says a diagnosis needs both a matching symptom pattern and breathing tests showing that airflow changes over time. A normal test on a good day doesn't rule it out. That passage was cut off before it finished describing cough alone.\n\n**Reflux** (source: \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD),\" general clinical reference): The typical signs are heartburn and acid or food coming back into the throat. These are often worse after meals, when bending forward, and when lying down. Chronic cough is listed as a less typical sign. The passage says it is much less specific, that many people with it turn out to have another cause, and that it responds less predictably to acid-reducing treatment.\n\nI can't tell from the guidance I found which of the two is more likely causing your cough. The passages describe each condition but don't compare them for someone who has both, and they don't say how the two interact in one person. Since your cough is worse at night and you have both conditions, it's worth raising with your clinician, who can work out which is more likely.\n\nNo emergency signs matched your description. That isn't a guarantee, though. If your breathing becomes severe, gets worse, or you develop new symptoms you can't explain, seek medical advice promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
